# Algorithms

## Determine the URL for matching a document

To determine the URL to use for matching a document, given the document, `match_origin_as_fallback` and `match_about_blank`:

1. Let |url| be the document's URL.
1. If the [=scheme=] of |url| is `http`, `https` or `file`:
    1. Return |url|.
1. If the [=scheme=] of |url| is `blob`, `data` or `filesystem`, or if |url| is `about:blank` or `about:srcdoc`:
    1. If `match_origin_as_fallback` is set to `true`:
        1. If the document's origin is a [=tuple origin=]:
            1. Let |document-origin| be the <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-serialisation-of-an-origin">serialization</a> of the document's origin.
            1. If the [=scheme=] of |document-origin| is `http`, `https` or `file`:
                1. Return |document-origin|.
            1. Else, return null.
        1.  Note: If not a [=tuple origin=], the document’s origin is an [=opaque origin=].
            1. Let |precursor-origin| be the <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-serialisation-of-an-origin">serialization</a> of the document’s precursor origin, if any.
                
                Issue: "precursor origin" concept needs to be specified. It is not in the HTML spec at the moment. At least Chrome and Firefox recognize the concept, see e.g. <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1715167">https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1715167</a>.
            1. If the [=scheme=] of |precursor-origin| is `http`, `https` or `file`:
                1. Return |precursor-origin|.
            1. Else, return null.
    1. Else, if `match_about_blank` is set to `true`:
        1. If |url| is `about:blank` or `about:srcdoc`:
            1. Let |opener| be the active document of document’s [=opener browsing context=].
            1. If all of the following conditions are true:
                - |opener| is not null
                - |opener|’s origin is still the same as the document’s [=opener origin at creation=]
                - The algorithm has not been repeated for |opener| yet.
                 
                 Then repeat the algorithm for |opener|.
1. Return null.

## Inject a content script

Issue: If the same extension specifies the same script twice, what should happen? ([bug](https://crbug.com/324096753))

To determine if a content script should be injected in a document:

1. Let |url| be the result of running [[#determine-the-url-for-matching-a-document]].
1. If the extension does not have access to |url|, return.
1. If |url| is not matched by a match pattern in `matches`, return.
1. If `include_globs` is present and |url| is not matched by any glob pattern, return.
1. If |url| matches an entry in `exclude_matches` or `exclude_globs`, return.
1. If this is a child frame, and `all_frames` is not `true`, return.
1. Otherwise, inject the content script. This should be done based on the `run_at` setting.

## Extension icon selection

### Icon selection precedence order
The user agent selects the icon from the first of these sources that yields an [=icon resource=]:

1. An icon set through an API. The API defines its scopes, which apply from most to least specific, for example instance, tab, window, then global.
    1. Prefer instance-specific if declared and supported (For example, `browser.notifications.create({iconUrl})`).
    1. Prefer tab-specific if declared and supported.
    1. Prefer window-specific if declared and supported.
    1. Prefer global if declared and supported.
1. Prefer API-specific declarative manifest values.
    1. Prefer `action.icon_variants`.
    1. Prefer `action.default_icon`.
1. Prefer generic declarative manifest values.
    1. Prefer `icon_variants`.
    1. Prefer `icons`.

Issue(1115): unspecified behavior for [=icon resource=] declarations which do not resolve to an actual extension path. In general if no valid [=icon resource=] can be extracted from a specific layer, not all implementations currently walk down the above specified precedence order.

### Selecting [=icon resource=] from [=icon variants=]
Before walking each [=icon group=] in [=icon variants=], the user agent first determines the different matching criteria which will be matched on. For example, the [=color scheme=] `dark`.

The user agent determines the media type of an [=icon resource=] from the file extension of its [=package path=], for example `image/png` for `.png` and `image/svg+xml` for `.svg`. An icon resource is supported when the user agent can decode that media type, and is a vector when that media type is resolution independent, such as `image/svg+xml`. A user agent must support `image/png` and should support `image/svg+xml`.

With these matching criteria, [=icon variants=] are walked through as such:

1. Take the first [=icon group=] in the list.
1. If the [=icon group=] is not of type object, go to next [=icon group=].
1. If the [=icon group=] has unknown keys, go to next [=icon group=] and optionally log a warning.
1. Walk through each matching criterion specified in the [=icon group=].
    1. Check if the matching criterion is a [=list=]. If not, go to next [=icon group=] and optionally log a warning.
    1. If the list of criterion values includes unknown values, ignore these and optionally log a warning.
    1. If the matching criterion does not include the pre-selected criterion, go to next [=icon group=].
    1. Otherwise, continue checking the next matching criterion.
1. Use [[#selecting-icon-size-resource-from-an-icon-group]] to determine the final icon resource to use. If this algorithm gives no valid icon resource, go to next [=icon group=].

Issue(1119): the current WebKit and Chromium implementations do not skip when encountering unknown keys. While the Orion implementation actively skips the [=icon group=] with unrecognized keys.
Issue: Orion does not skip blocks with only SVG icons while it does not support SVG icons properly currently.

The first fully supported [=icon group=] is selected for use as the [=extension icons=].

If no [=icon group=] yields an [=icon resource=], the user agent continues with the next source in [[#icon-selection-precedence-order]].

Issue: User agents differ on whether a lone [=icon group=] is used whatever its matching criteria, and on whether criteria are dropped when no group matches.

### Selecting icon size resource from an [=icon group=]

Walk through each icon resource:

1. Discard each unsupported icon resource.
1. Determine if the icon resource is a vector.

The user agent uses the icon selection algorithm to determine the final [=icon resource=] to use.

#### Icon selection algorithm
Sort the icon resources by size, from smallest to largest. When sorting, the `any` keyword is regarded as the largest possible size.

If no icon resource is supported, the [=icon group=] yields no [=icon resource=].

If only a single icon resource is supported select this icon resource. Otherwise:

Determine lookup size:

1. Let |size| be the size, in device-independent pixels, at which the user agent renders the icon.
1. If every icon resource is a vector and the group comes from [=icon variants=], let |lookup size| be |size|, because a vector renders sharply at any scale.
1. Otherwise, let |lookup size| be |size| multiplied by the device pixel ratio of the display.

For example, the user agent renders a 16 pixel icon on a 2x display. The lookup size is 16 for vector resources and 32 for all other resources.

Issue: WebKit and Firefox scale the lookup size by the device pixel ratio for vector resources too.

Walk through the sorted icon sizes and return the first [=icon resource=] whose size is equal to or larger than the lookup size. If there is none, return the icon resource with the largest size.

Issue(949): User agents differ on whether `any` is preferred over a numeric size or used only when no numeric size is large enough.

